{"labs": [{"_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}]}], "type": "scholar", "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0}, {"id": 1, "title": "", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"text": "", "id": 0, "time": "0", "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "According to Bruce Tuckman, there are five phases of group development: forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning.  Groups can go back and forth between the phases, so they should not be seen as sequential.</br></br>Stage 1: In the forming stage, it is said by Tuckman that team members will look to the team leader for direction and supervision. Being accepted in the group is key at this stage, so group members will observe each other carefully. Members learn about the job before them and how to deal with one another.", "id": 1, "time": "100", "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img01.jpg"}, {"text": "Stage 2:  The next stage is called storming, having been characterized by conflict and competition. As team members attempt to organize for the task, it has been suggested that questions arise among the group. These questions will result in conflicts over structure, leadership, authority and power.</br></br>Stage 3: In the norming stage, members are willing to change their preconceived opinions in response to the ideas and facts introduced by the other members of the team. Cliques disappear and leadership is shared. Having been accepted by the group, members start to warm up to one another. A growing level of trust fosters group cohesion. At this point, it has been agreed upon to solicit and share feelings, accept constructive feedback, and explore the range of possible actions related to the task.", "id": 2, "time": "28000", "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img02.jpg"}, {"text": "Stage 4: The next stage, the performing stage, is difficult to attain. Members may work independently, in small groups, or all together. Their actions are well adjusted to the needs of the group. It is decided to place emphasis on achievement through problem-solving, hard work and productivity.</br></br>Stage 5: The final stage, adjourning, was suggested by Tuckman to include the disengagement from the task and the conclusion of relationships. Being given the opportunity, members say their personal goodbyes to each other.  Having been recognized for their participation and achievement, the team is disbanded.", "id": 3, "time": "70000", "image": "engenn11le06ob09re2img03.jpg"}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le06ob09re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le06ob09re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "_data": [], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1}], "exercises": [{"id": 0, "stimuli_ids": [0], "questions": [], "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can discuss reasons and results of an event or action related to work and jobs.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk or report about things, actions, events, or people in a work environment.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about group development.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "Why do you think it is hard to work in a group sometimes? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Do you think there are different stages that a group goes through? Why or why not?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"correct_feedback": "Correct", "allowed_attempts": 2, "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}], "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask your partner:", "type": "free_header_text"}, {"content": " How many phases are there in group development? ", "type": "free_type